I boundaries\n\nThese are not error rows because the C implementation does not reject them:\n\n| Boundary | C behavior | Coverage |\n|----------|------------|----------|\n| `stbds_hash_bytes(NULL, 0, seed)` | Valid: no pointer dereference; returns the empty-input hash. | [ ] |\n| Zero length with a non-null pointer | Valid: returns the empty-input hash. | [ ] |\n| Large length backed by an equally large allocation | Valid: no documented maximum; hashes all bytes. | [ ] |\n| Null pointer with positive length | Undefined behavior from dereferencing `NULL`; no stable C result exists to compare. | N/A |\n| Length larger than the pointed-to allocation | Undefined behavior from an out-of-bounds read; no stable C result exists to compare. | N/A |\n| Out-of-range enum | Neither public entry point accepts an enum. | N/A |\n| One past documented range | No input range is documented or checked. | N/A |\n\nUndefined-behavior cases are intentionally not invoked by the test process.\n# Configuration Surface\n\nThe built C library uses 64-bit `size_t`, so one complete input word is eight\nbytes. `stbds_hash_bytes` branches on:\n\n1. whether the complete-word loop executes zero, one, or multiple times; and\n2. the exact tail size selected by `switch (len - i)`, from 0 through 7.\n\nThe table is that mechanically derived cross-product. Every hash row covers\nrandom byte contents (including bytes below and above `0x80`), random seeds\n(including `0` and `SIZE_MAX`), and pointer offsets 0 through 7. Multiple-word\nrows also include large valid buffers because C defines no maximum length.\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 1 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | zero complete words + 0 tail bytes (`len = 0`); null and non-null pointers | [ ] |\n| 2 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | zero complete words + 1 tail byte (`len = 1`) | [ ] |\n| 3 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | zero complete words + 2 tail bytes (`len = 2`) | [ ] |\n| 4 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | zero complete words + 3 tail bytes (`len = 3`) | [ ] |\n| 5 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | zero complete words + 4 tail bytes (`len = 4`) | [ ] |\n| 6 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | zero complete words + 5 tail bytes (`len = 5`) | [ ] |\n| 7 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | zero complete words + 6 tail bytes (`len = 6`) | [ ] |\n| 8 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | zero complete words + 7 tail bytes (`len = 7`) | [ ] |\n| 9 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | one complete word + 0 tail bytes (`len = 8`) | [ ] |\n| 10 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | one complete word + 1 tail byte (`len = 9`) | [ ] |\n| 11 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | one complete word + 2 tail bytes (`len = 10`) | [ ] |\n| 12 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | one complete word + 3 tail bytes (`len = 11`) | [ ] |\n| 13 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | one complete word + 4 tail bytes (`len = 12`) | [ ] |\n| 14 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | one complete word + 5 tail bytes (`len = 13`) | [ ] |\n| 15 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | one complete word + 6 tail bytes (`len = 14`) | [ ] |\n| 16 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | one complete word + 7 tail bytes (`len = 15`) | [ ] |\n| 17 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | multiple complete words + 0 tail bytes (`len = 8k`, `k >= 2`) | [ ] |\n| 18 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | multiple complete words + 1 tail byte (`len = 8k + 1`, `k >= 2`) | [ ] |\n| 19 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | multiple complete words + 2 tail bytes (`len = 8k + 2`, `k >= 2`) | [ ] |\n| 20 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | multiple complete words + 3 tail bytes (`len = 8k + 3`, `k >= 2`) | [ ] |\n| 21 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | multiple complete words + 4 tail bytes (`len = 8k + 4`, `k >= 2`) | [ ] |\n| 22 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | multiple complete words + 5 tail bytes (`len = 8k + 5`, `k >= 2`) | [ ] |\n| 23 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | multiple complete words + 6 tail bytes (`len = 8k + 6`, `k >= 2`) | [ ] |\n| 24 | `stbds_hash_bytes` | multiple complete words + 7 tail bytes (`len = 8k + 7`, `k >= 2`) | [ ] |\n| 25 | `siphash` | full operation: generate 64 wrapping bytes from any `int init` that avoids signed-overflow UB, hash lengths 0 through 63, and print all 64 rows | [ ] |\n\n## Other axes\n\n| Axis | C treatment |\n|------|-------------|\n| Byte order | Input words and output bytes are explicitly assembled little-endian. |\n| Element type | Input is always treated as `unsigned char *`. |\n| Seed | `size_t`; no mode branch or restricted value. |\n| `siphash` initialization | `int`; no mode branch or restricted value, except that signed overflow is undefined C behavior. |\n| Runtime options, modes, flags | None. |\n| Compile-time feature branches | None. |\n| Rust Cargo features | None declared. |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
